TOMBSTONE 01 ~~> THE VOID THAT COULD NOT MAKE NOISE
════════════════════════════════════════════════════
WHAT IT WAS
  A planted known-bad: a "spilling" burrow meant to BREAK orthogonality,
  so the commutator would catch it. Its x-shift was keyed off the
  RESULTING z:  BURROW_SPILL(s,dz) = { x + (s.z+dz >= 3 ? 1 : 0), z+dz }

HOW IT DIED
  Run before the page was built. It read ZERO — it did not make noise.
  MOVE never touches z, so both orders (MOVE-then-burrow, burrow-then-MOVE)
  reach the SAME final z, both fire the same shift, and the commutator
  cancelled to zero. The "bad" operator was not actually order-sensitive.

WHY IT MATTERS
  A known-bad that keys off a quantity both orders AGREE on cannot be
  caught by a commutator. To break orthogonality the fault must depend
  on what the two orders DISAGREE about — here, x (which MOVE changes).

THE REPLACEMENT
  BURROW_SPILL(s,dz) = { x + floor(x/10), z+dz }  ~~> keyed off x.
  MOVE-first reads the moved x; burrow-first reads the original.
  They disagree when x crosses a /10 boundary: 12 vs 11. Non-zero. Caught.

THE LESSON
  This is the house rule proving itself: an instrument whose good news is
  silence needs a known-bad that MUST make noise — AND the known-bad has
  to be checked too, because a broken known-bad is silent for the wrong
  reason and looks identical to success.
